//! The JSON module: a parser and an inspector, over a flat node store.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.3 called this "the most valuable module for proving the language, because it
//! exercises tagged unions, containers and strings together", and item 4 in W7's order. It is reachable now
//! that `#must` exists (ADR-0151), because a parse that can fail needs a way to say that ignoring the
//! failure is an error.
//!
//! # Why a flat node store rather than a recursive value type
//!
//! A JSON value is recursive: an array holds values, an object holds named values. The obvious shape is a
//! `variant` whose cases include a list of itself — and that needs **indirection**, because a type cannot
//! contain itself by value. So the choice is between a pointer per node and an **index** per node, and this
//! module takes the index:
//!
//! ```text
//!   document.nodes: [..]Json_Node        one growable array, one allocation policy
//!   a value        : s64                 an index into it, or NONE
//!   children       : first / next        a sibling chain of indices
//! ```
//!
//! Three reasons, in the order they mattered. **Freeing is one call**: `free_document` releases the array
//! and every string in it, where a pointer tree needs a recursive walk that has to be right on the error
//! path too. **A handle is copyable and comparable**: an `s64` can be stored, compared and passed without
//! anybody wondering who owns it, and `NONE` is a real absent value rather than a null nobody checked.
//! And **it is what fast parsers do** — a tape of nodes is cache-friendly in the order a parse produces
//! and a walk consumes, though that is a claim this project does not measure (ADR-0146 §4) and so is third
//! rather than first.
//!
//! Rejected: **a `variant` of cases with `*Json` children**, the textbook DOM. It reads better at the type
//! level and is worse everywhere else: a recursive free, an ownership question at every field, and a
//! partial tree to unwind when a parse fails halfway. Rejected: **a `Map` per object**, which is
//! `Map(s64, s64)` today and so cannot key on a string at all — and would be slower than a linear scan for
//! the three-to-five keys a real object usually has. Members are a sibling chain, scanned in order, which
//! also **preserves source order**, something a hash map destroys and a diff-minded caller wants.
//!
//! # Why numbers go through `strtod`
//!
//! A JSON number is a decimal in the source and a `float64` in the value model, and converting between
//! them **exactly** is a genuinely hard problem — the reason `Math` ships no `sqrt` (ADR-0112) applies with
//! more force here, because a wrong last bit in a parsed number is a wrong *answer* rather than a wrong
//! approximation. So the platform's correctly-rounded `strtod` does it, which ADR-0114 made reachable by
//! letting a float cross the `#foreign` boundary.
//!
//! Integers are converted **in Jairs**, not by `strtod`, and that is not an inconsistency: an integer's
//! conversion is exact arithmetic with no rounding question, `s64` is what a caller indexing or counting
//! wants, and going through `float64` would silently lose precision above 2^53. So a number carries both —
//! `number_of` for the float, `integer_of` for the exact integer when the source had no fraction or
//! exponent, and `is_integer` to ask.
//!
//! **No serialisation.** Writing a document back out needs to render a `float64` as the shortest decimal
//! that round-trips, which is a correct `dtoa` — and an approximate one would emit numbers this module
//! could not read back, which is worse than emitting none. `String`'s allocating half is ready for the rest
//! of it, so this is a wave's work behind a single missing piece, and it is named rather than half-built.
//!
//! **No streaming or incremental parse.** The whole text is in memory already, since a Jairs `string` is
//! data and a count; a streaming parser answers a different question and would need its own state machine.
//!
//! # A caller installs an allocator first
//!
//! Parsed strings come from `context.allocator` and are released by `String.free_string`, so a caller
//! installs one before calling `parse` — the convention ADR-0062 set for `String`'s allocating half, and
//! forgetting is not silent: `context.allocator` is null until set, and a call through it traps with a
//! source location.
//!
//! The **node store** is `malloc`ed instead, matching `List` and `Map`. That split is the library's, not
//! this module's — `String` allocates through the context and the containers do not — and JSON is the first
//! module that needs both halves, so it is the first place the seam is visible. Recorded rather than
//! papered over: unifying it is a decision about every container at once.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! **No duplicate-key policy.** A duplicate key is kept, both copies, in source order — `member` finds the
//! first. JSON itself does not say what a duplicate means, so inventing a rule here would be inventing
//! semantics; a caller who cares can walk the chain.

#import "Basic";
#import "String";

/// The absent index — no first child, no next sibling, no such member.
///
/// `-1` rather than `0`, because `0` is the root's own index in every non-empty document and a sentinel
/// that collides with a real value is the bug this constant exists to prevent.
NONE :: -1;

/// What one node is.
///
/// `NULL_`, `TRUE_` and `FALSE_` carry no payload, so the kind *is* the value. They are three kinds rather
/// than one `BOOLEAN` with a flag because a `switch` over a kind then needs no second question, and because
/// `null` is not a boolean.
///
/// The trailing underscores are there because `null`, `true` and `false` are not available as bare member
/// names in the surface this module can spell today; recorded rather than silently renamed, so a reader
/// knows it is a spelling constraint and not a taste.
Json_Kind :: enum {
    /// `null`.
    NULL_;
    /// `true`.
    TRUE_;
    /// `false`.
    FALSE_;
    /// A number. `number` holds it; `integer` too when `is_integer` is true.
    NUMBER;
    /// A string. `text` holds it, unescaped, and the document owns it.
    STRING;
    /// An array. `first` is its first element, or `NONE`.
    ARRAY;
    /// An object. `first` is its first member, or `NONE`; each member's `key` is set.
    OBJECT;
}

/// One value in the store.
///
/// A struct with every field present rather than a `variant`, and that is a deliberate reversal of the
/// module's own preference for tagged unions: a variant would refuse a read of the wrong case, which is
/// exactly right — but the *parser* fills fields in an order the tag does not yet know, and a node is 72
/// bytes either way once a `string` and a `float64` are in it. So the tag is advisory here and the
/// accessors below do the checking a variant would have done, returning a stated default rather than
/// trapping, because a caller inspecting untrusted JSON is asking a question rather than asserting a fact.
Json_Node :: struct {
    /// Which kind this is.
    kind: Json_Kind;
    /// The number, when `kind` is `NUMBER`.
    number: float64;
    /// The exact integer, when `kind` is `NUMBER` and `is_integer` is true.
    integer: s64;
    /// Whether the source number had no fraction and no exponent, so `integer` is exact.
    is_integer: bool;
    /// The string, when `kind` is `STRING`. Owned by the document.
    text: string;
    /// This member's name, when the parent is an `OBJECT`. Owned by the document.
    key: string;
    /// The first child, when `kind` is `ARRAY` or `OBJECT`; `NONE` when empty.
    first: s64;
    /// The next sibling under the same parent; `NONE` when last.
    next: s64;
}

/// A parsed document: the node store, plus the root's index.
///
/// The document owns every node and every string in them. `free_document` is the only release, and it is
/// the *whole* release — there is nothing else for a caller to remember.
Json_Document :: struct {
    /// Every node, in the order the parse produced them. Read through `view(nodes.data, nodes.count)`,
    /// because a `[..]T` cannot be indexed directly.
    nodes: [..]Json_Node;
    /// The root value's index, or `NONE` when the parse failed.
    root: s64;
    /// The byte offset where the parse stopped, when it failed. `-1` on success.
    ///
    /// An offset rather than a message, because a message would need formatting this module cannot do
    /// (see the docs above) and an offset is what a caller renders against their own source anyway.
    error_at: s64;
}

/// Parses `text` into a document.
///
/// `#must`: the result carries a success flag beside the document, and ignoring it has to be *written*
/// (ADR-0151). A parse of untrusted input is the case that marker exists for — a caller who ignores the
/// flag reads `root == NONE` as an empty document and gets silently wrong answers.
///
/// On failure the document is still valid and still needs freeing: whatever nodes the parse built before it
/// stopped are in it, `root` is `NONE`, and `error_at` is the byte offset. Freeing a failed parse the same
/// way as a successful one is the point of the flat store.
parse :: (text: string) -> (Json_Document, bool) #must {
    doc: Json_Document;
    doc.root = NONE;
    doc.error_at = -1;

    at := skip_space(text, 0);
    value, ok, after := parse_value(*doc, text, at);
    if !ok {
        doc.error_at = after;
        return doc, false;
    }

    // **Trailing content is an error**, not ignored. `{"a":1} garbage` is not a JSON document, and a parser
    // that accepted it would let a truncated stream through as a valid prefix.
    rest := skip_space(text, after);
    if rest != text.count {
        doc.error_at = rest;
        return doc, false;
    }

    doc.root = value;
    return doc, true;
}

/// Releases every node and every string the document owns.
///
/// Safe on a failed parse and safe to call twice: the array is emptied, so the second call walks nothing.
free_document :: (doc: *Json_Document) {
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    i := 0;
    while i < doc.nodes.count {
        if !is_empty(nodes[i].text) {
            free_string(nodes[i].text);
        }
        if !is_empty(nodes[i].key) {
            free_string(nodes[i].key);
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if doc.nodes.data != null {
        free(untyped(doc.nodes.data));
    }
    doc.nodes.data = null;
    doc.nodes.count = 0;
    doc.nodes.capacity = 0;
    doc.root = NONE;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Inspecting
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The kind of node `at`, or `NULL_` when `at` is not a node in this document.
///
/// `NULL_` for an out-of-range index rather than a trap, because every accessor here answers a *question*
/// about possibly-absent data — `kind_of(member(doc, obj, "missing"))` should say "nothing there", not stop
/// the program. A caller who wants a trap can compare against `NONE` first.
kind_of :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> Json_Kind {
    if !in_range(doc, at) {
        return Json_Kind.NULL_;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    return nodes[at].kind;
}

/// The number at `at`, or `0.0` when it is not a number.
number_of :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> float64 {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.NUMBER {
        return 0.0;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    return nodes[at].number;
}

/// The exact integer at `at`, or `0` when it is not an integral number.
///
/// Separate from `number_of` because going through `float64` loses precision above 2^53, and a caller
/// indexing or counting wants the exact value. `is_integer_at` asks whether this is meaningful.
integer_of :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> s64 {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.NUMBER {
        return 0;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    if !nodes[at].is_integer {
        return 0;
    }
    return nodes[at].integer;
}

/// Whether `at` is a number whose source had no fraction and no exponent.
is_integer_at :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> bool {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.NUMBER {
        return false;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    return nodes[at].is_integer;
}

/// The string at `at`, or the empty string when it is not a string.
///
/// **Borrowed, not copied**: the document owns it and `free_document` releases it, so a caller who needs it
/// to outlive the document must `concat` or `substring` it. Returning a copy would make every read allocate
/// and every caller free, which is the wrong default for an inspector.
string_of :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> string {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.STRING {
        return "";
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    return nodes[at].text;
}

/// Whether `at` is `true`. `false` for `false`, and for everything that is not a boolean.
is_true :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> bool {
    return kind_of(doc, at) == Json_Kind.TRUE_;
}

/// How many elements the array at `at` has, or `0` when it is not an array.
///
/// Walks the sibling chain, so it is `O(n)`. A cached count per node would make it `O(1)` and cost eight
/// bytes on every node including the scalars; the chain walk is what an `array_at` loop does anyway, so a
/// caller iterating pays the same either way and a caller merely asking is rare.
array_count :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> s64 {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.ARRAY {
        return 0;
    }
    return chain_length(doc, at);
}

/// Element `index` of the array at `at`, or `NONE`.
array_at :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64, index: s64) -> s64 {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.ARRAY {
        return NONE;
    }
    return chain_at(doc, at, index);
}

/// How many members the object at `at` has, or `0` when it is not an object.
member_count :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> s64 {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.OBJECT {
        return 0;
    }
    return chain_length(doc, at);
}

/// Member `index` of the object at `at`, in **source order**, or `NONE`.
///
/// Source order is preserved because members are a chain rather than a hash table — which is the property a
/// caller comparing two documents, or round-tripping one, needs.
member_at :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64, index: s64) -> s64 {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.OBJECT {
        return NONE;
    }
    return chain_at(doc, at, index);
}

/// The name of the member node `at`, or the empty string. Borrowed, like `string_of`.
key_of :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> string {
    if !in_range(doc, at) {
        return "";
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    return nodes[at].key;
}

/// The first member of the object at `at` named `name`, or `NONE`.
///
/// **First**, because JSON does not say what a duplicate key means and this module refuses to invent a rule;
/// both copies are in the document and a caller who cares can walk `member_at`.
///
/// A linear scan. An object with three keys is faster to scan than to hash, and `Map` cannot key on a
/// string today in any case (it is `Map(s64, s64)`).
member :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64, name: string) -> s64 {
    if kind_of(doc, at) != Json_Kind.OBJECT {
        return NONE;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    child := nodes[at].first;
    while child != NONE {
        if equal(nodes[child].key, name) {
            return child;
        }
        child = nodes[child].next;
    }
    return NONE;
}

/// Whether the object at `at` has a member named `name`.
has_member :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64, name: string) -> bool {
    return member(doc, at, name) != NONE;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Internals
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Whether `at` names a node in this document.
in_range :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> bool {
    if at < 0 {
        return false;
    }
    return at < doc.nodes.count;
}

/// How many nodes are in the chain under `at`.
chain_length :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64) -> s64 {
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    n := 0;
    child := nodes[at].first;
    while child != NONE {
        n = n + 1;
        child = nodes[child].next;
    }
    return n;
}

/// The `index`th node in the chain under `at`, or `NONE`.
chain_at :: (doc: *Json_Document, at: s64, index: s64) -> s64 {
    if index < 0 {
        return NONE;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    child := nodes[at].first;
    i := 0;
    while child != NONE {
        if i == index {
            return child;
        }
        i = i + 1;
        child = nodes[child].next;
    }
    return NONE;
}

/// Appends a node and returns its index, or `NONE` when it cannot grow.
///
/// Doubling from four, matching `List` (ADR-0107) and `Bucket_Array`'s spine: a fixed increment makes a
/// sequence of appends `O(n²)`.
add_node :: (doc: *Json_Document, kind: Json_Kind) -> s64 {
    if doc.nodes.count == doc.nodes.capacity {
        grown := 4;
        if doc.nodes.capacity > 0 {
            grown = doc.nodes.capacity * 2;
        }
        fresh := typed(Json_Node, malloc(grown * size_of(Json_Node)));
        if fresh == null {
            return NONE;
        }
        old := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
        new := view(fresh, doc.nodes.count);
        i := 0;
        while i < doc.nodes.count {
            new[i] = old[i];
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if doc.nodes.data != null {
            free(untyped(doc.nodes.data));
        }
        doc.nodes.data = fresh;
        doc.nodes.capacity = grown;
    }
    slots := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.capacity);
    at := doc.nodes.count;
    fresh_node: Json_Node;
    fresh_node.kind = kind;
    fresh_node.number = 0.0;
    fresh_node.integer = 0;
    fresh_node.is_integer = false;
    fresh_node.text = "";
    fresh_node.key = "";
    fresh_node.first = NONE;
    fresh_node.next = NONE;
    slots[at] = fresh_node;
    doc.nodes.count = doc.nodes.count + 1;
    return at;
}

/// The first offset at or after `from` that is not JSON whitespace.
///
/// JSON's whitespace is exactly space, tab, newline and carriage return — not `isspace`'s set, which
/// includes vertical tab and form feed. Written out rather than delegated, because "whatever the C library
/// thinks is space" is not the grammar.
skip_space :: (text: string, from: s64) -> s64 {
    at := from;
    while at < text.count {
        c := byte_at(text, at);
        if c != 32 {
            if c != 9 {
                if c != 10 {
                    if c != 13 {
                        return at;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        at = at + 1;
    }
    return at;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Parsing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Parses one value starting at `at`.
///
/// Returns its node index, whether it parsed, and the offset just past it — or, on failure, the offset
/// where it went wrong, which is what `parse` reports as `error_at`. Three results rather than a struct,
/// because ADR-0052 gave this language multiple returns and a one-use struct would be a name for nothing.
///
/// Recursive descent, so nesting depth is stack depth. **No depth limit**, deliberately: the limit that
/// matters is the caller's stack, a cap would be an arbitrary number in a library, and a caller parsing
/// untrusted input has to bound the *input size* anyway, which bounds the depth. Recorded because the
/// absence looks like an oversight otherwise.
parse_value :: (doc: *Json_Document, text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool, s64) {
    if at >= text.count {
        return NONE, false, at;
    }
    c := byte_at(text, at);

    // `{` and `[` first, because they recurse and the rest are leaves.
    if c == 123 {
        return parse_object(doc, text, at);
    }
    if c == 91 {
        return parse_array(doc, text, at);
    }
    if c == 34 {
        return parse_string(doc, text, at);
    }
    if literal_at(text, at, "true") {
        node := add_node(doc, Json_Kind.TRUE_);
        return node, node != NONE, at + 4;
    }
    if literal_at(text, at, "false") {
        node := add_node(doc, Json_Kind.FALSE_);
        return node, node != NONE, at + 5;
    }
    if literal_at(text, at, "null") {
        node := add_node(doc, Json_Kind.NULL_);
        return node, node != NONE, at + 4;
    }
    // A number, or nothing. `-` and the digits are the only remaining legal starts; JSON has no leading
    // `+`, no bare `.5`, and no `Infinity` or `NaN`, and each of those absences is the grammar rather than
    // a simplification here.
    if c == 45 {
        return parse_number(doc, text, at);
    }
    if c >= 48 {
        if c <= 57 {
            return parse_number(doc, text, at);
        }
    }
    return NONE, false, at;
}

/// Whether `text` has exactly `word` at `at`.
literal_at :: (text: string, at: s64, word: string) -> bool {
    if at + word.count > text.count {
        return false;
    }
    i := 0;
    while i < word.count {
        if byte_at(text, at + i) != byte_at(word, i) {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Parses `[ … ]`, building a sibling chain.
///
/// The chain is built by remembering the **last** element and writing its `next`, rather than by walking to
/// the end each time — which would make an n-element array `O(n²)` for no reason. Written out because the
/// `last` variable looks redundant until you notice the alternative.
parse_array :: (doc: *Json_Document, text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool, s64) {
    node := add_node(doc, Json_Kind.ARRAY);
    if node == NONE {
        return NONE, false, at;
    }
    cursor := skip_space(text, at + 1);
    if cursor < text.count {
        if byte_at(text, cursor) == 93 {
            return node, true, cursor + 1;
        }
    }

    last := NONE;
    while true {
        element, ok, after := parse_value(doc, text, cursor);
        if !ok {
            return NONE, false, after;
        }
        // Re-viewed after every `parse_value`, because a nested parse may have **grown the store** and
        // moved it. A view held across an append is a dangling read, and this is exactly where the flat
        // store's one sharp edge is — so it is re-taken rather than hoisted, in all four places.
        nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
        if last == NONE {
            nodes[node].first = element;
        } else {
            nodes[last].next = element;
        }
        last = element;

        cursor = skip_space(text, after);
        if cursor >= text.count {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        c := byte_at(text, cursor);
        if c == 93 {
            return node, true, cursor + 1;
        }
        if c != 44 {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        cursor = skip_space(text, cursor + 1);
        // A trailing comma is an error: `[1,]` is not JSON. Checked here rather than by letting
        // `parse_value` fail on `]`, so the reported offset is the comma's own position.
        if cursor < text.count {
            if byte_at(text, cursor) == 93 {
                return NONE, false, cursor;
            }
        }
    }
    return NONE, false, cursor;
}

/// Parses `{ "k": v, … }`, building a sibling chain whose nodes carry their keys.
///
/// A member is the **value's own node** with `key` set, rather than a separate pair node. One node fewer per
/// member, and `member(doc, obj, "k")` returns something a caller can read directly with `string_of` or
/// `number_of` — where a pair node would make every read go through a `value_of` nobody wants.
parse_object :: (doc: *Json_Document, text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool, s64) {
    node := add_node(doc, Json_Kind.OBJECT);
    if node == NONE {
        return NONE, false, at;
    }
    cursor := skip_space(text, at + 1);
    if cursor < text.count {
        if byte_at(text, cursor) == 125 {
            return node, true, cursor + 1;
        }
    }

    last := NONE;
    while true {
        if cursor >= text.count {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        if byte_at(text, cursor) != 34 {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        name, name_ok, after_name := scan_string(text, cursor);
        if !name_ok {
            return NONE, false, after_name;
        }

        cursor = skip_space(text, after_name);
        if cursor >= text.count {
            free_string(name);
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        if byte_at(text, cursor) != 58 {
            free_string(name);
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        cursor = skip_space(text, cursor + 1);

        value, ok, after := parse_value(doc, text, cursor);
        if !ok {
            free_string(name);
            return NONE, false, after;
        }
        nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
        nodes[value].key = name;
        if last == NONE {
            nodes[node].first = value;
        } else {
            nodes[last].next = value;
        }
        last = value;

        cursor = skip_space(text, after);
        if cursor >= text.count {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        c := byte_at(text, cursor);
        if c == 125 {
            return node, true, cursor + 1;
        }
        if c != 44 {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        cursor = skip_space(text, cursor + 1);
        if cursor < text.count {
            if byte_at(text, cursor) == 125 {
                return NONE, false, cursor;
            }
        }
    }
    return NONE, false, cursor;
}

/// Parses a string value into a node.
parse_string :: (doc: *Json_Document, text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool, s64) {
    value, ok, after := scan_string(text, at);
    if !ok {
        return NONE, false, after;
    }
    node := add_node(doc, Json_Kind.STRING);
    if node == NONE {
        free_string(value);
        return NONE, false, at;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    nodes[node].text = value;
    return node, true, after;
}

/// Reads a quoted string starting at `at` and returns it **unescaped and allocated**.
///
/// Two passes: measure, then fill. One pass into a growable buffer would double-copy on every growth, and
/// the measured length is exact because unescaping only ever *shrinks* — `\n` is two bytes in and one out.
///
/// **`\uXXXX` becomes UTF-8**, which is the one piece of real work here: JSON escapes a code point as
/// UTF-16, so a surrogate pair has to be recombined before encoding. A lone surrogate is an error rather
/// than a replacement character, because silently substituting U+FFFD turns malformed input into a valid
/// document that says something the source did not.
scan_string :: (text: string, at: s64) -> (string, bool, s64) {
    if at >= text.count {
        return "", false, at;
    }
    if byte_at(text, at) != 34 {
        return "", false, at;
    }

    // Pass one: how long is the unescaped result, and where does the literal end?
    size := 0;
    cursor := at + 1;
    while cursor < text.count {
        c := byte_at(text, cursor);
        if c == 34 {
            break;
        }
        if c == 92 {
            width, ok := escape_width(text, cursor);
            if !ok {
                return "", false, cursor;
            }
            size = size + width;
            cursor = cursor + escape_length(text, cursor);
        } else {
            // A raw control byte is invalid in a JSON string. Refused rather than passed through, because a
            // parser that accepts one produces a document whose text cannot be re-emitted as JSON.
            if c < 32 {
                return "", false, cursor;
            }
            size = size + 1;
            cursor = cursor + 1;
        }
    }
    if cursor >= text.count {
        return "", false, cursor;
    }
    let_end := cursor;

    // Pass two: fill.
    //
    // **Through `context.allocator`, not `malloc`**, because the result is handed to `String.adopt` and
    // released by `String.free_string`, which frees through `context.allocator_free`. Allocating with
    // `malloc` here would pair a libc allocation with a context free — invisible while the installed
    // allocator *is* libc, and corruption the moment a caller installs an arena. The library has a real seam
    // here, named in the module docs: `List` and `Map` allocate with `malloc`, `String` through the context,
    // and this module is the first that needs both.
    data := context.allocator(size + 1);
    if data == null {
        return "", false, at;
    }
    out := view(data, size + 1);
    write_at := 0;
    cursor = at + 1;
    while cursor < let_end {
        c := byte_at(text, cursor);
        if c == 92 {
            written := write_escape(text, cursor, out, write_at);
            write_at = write_at + written;
            cursor = cursor + escape_length(text, cursor);
        } else {
            out[write_at] = cast(u8, c);
            write_at = write_at + 1;
            cursor = cursor + 1;
        }
    }
    out[write_at] = cast(u8, 0);
    return adopt(data, write_at), true, let_end + 1;
}

/// How many bytes the escape at `at` produces, and whether it is a legal escape.
escape_width :: (text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
    if at + 1 >= text.count {
        return 0, false;
    }
    c := byte_at(text, at + 1);
    if c == 117 {
        point, ok := unicode_escape(text, at);
        if !ok {
            return 0, false;
        }
        return utf8_width(point), true;
    }
    // The seven single-character escapes JSON has, and no others: `\a` is not JSON even though C has it.
    if c == 34 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    if c == 92 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    if c == 47 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    if c == 98 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    if c == 102 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    if c == 110 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    if c == 114 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    if c == 116 {
        return 1, true;
    }
    return 0, false;
}

/// How many **source** bytes the escape at `at` occupies.
escape_length :: (text: string, at: s64) -> s64 {
    if at + 1 >= text.count {
        return 1;
    }
    if byte_at(text, at + 1) != 117 {
        return 2;
    }
    // `\uXXXX`, or twelve for a surrogate pair — the pair is one escape as far as the caller is concerned,
    // because it produces one code point.
    if surrogate_pair_at(text, at) {
        return 12;
    }
    return 6;
}

/// Whether the escape at `at` is a high surrogate followed by a `\u` low surrogate.
surrogate_pair_at :: (text: string, at: s64) -> bool {
    high, ok := hex4(text, at + 2);
    if !ok {
        return false;
    }
    if high < 55296 {
        return false;
    }
    if high > 56319 {
        return false;
    }
    if at + 11 >= text.count {
        return false;
    }
    if byte_at(text, at + 6) != 92 {
        return false;
    }
    if byte_at(text, at + 7) != 117 {
        return false;
    }
    low, low_ok := hex4(text, at + 8);
    if !low_ok {
        return false;
    }
    if low < 56320 {
        return false;
    }
    return low <= 57343;
}

/// The code point the `\u` escape at `at` denotes.
unicode_escape :: (text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
    value, ok := hex4(text, at + 2);
    if !ok {
        return 0, false;
    }
    if value < 55296 {
        return value, true;
    }
    if value > 57343 {
        return value, true;
    }
    // A surrogate. Only a well-formed high-then-low pair is legal; a lone one is an error.
    if value > 56319 {
        return 0, false;
    }
    if !surrogate_pair_at(text, at) {
        return 0, false;
    }
    low, low_ok := hex4(text, at + 8);
    if !low_ok {
        return 0, false;
    }
    return 65536 + (value - 55296) * 1024 + (low - 56320), true;
}

/// Four hex digits at `at`, as a number.
hex4 :: (text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
    if at + 4 > text.count {
        return 0, false;
    }
    value := 0;
    i := 0;
    while i < 4 {
        digit, ok := hex_digit(byte_at(text, at + i));
        if !ok {
            return 0, false;
        }
        value = value * 16 + digit;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return value, true;
}

/// One hex digit's value.
hex_digit :: (c: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
    if c >= 48 {
        if c <= 57 {
            return c - 48, true;
        }
    }
    if c >= 97 {
        if c <= 102 {
            return c - 97 + 10, true;
        }
    }
    if c >= 65 {
        if c <= 70 {
            return c - 65 + 10, true;
        }
    }
    return 0, false;
}

/// How many bytes `point` needs in UTF-8.
utf8_width :: (point: s64) -> s64 {
    if point < 128 {
        return 1;
    }
    if point < 2048 {
        return 2;
    }
    if point < 65536 {
        return 3;
    }
    return 4;
}

/// Writes the escape at `at` into `out` starting at `write_at`, and returns how many bytes it wrote.
write_escape :: (text: string, at: s64, out: []u8, write_at: s64) -> s64 {
    c := byte_at(text, at + 1);
    if c == 117 {
        point, ok := unicode_escape(text, at);
        if !ok {
            return 0;
        }
        return write_utf8(out, write_at, point);
    }
    out[write_at] = cast(u8, single_escape(c));
    return 1;
}

/// The byte a single-character escape produces.
single_escape :: (c: s64) -> s64 {
    if c == 98 {
        return 8;
    }
    if c == 102 {
        return 12;
    }
    if c == 110 {
        return 10;
    }
    if c == 114 {
        return 13;
    }
    if c == 116 {
        return 9;
    }
    // `"`, `\` and `/` all stand for themselves.
    return c;
}

/// Encodes `point` as UTF-8 at `write_at`, returning how many bytes it wrote.
write_utf8 :: (out: []u8, write_at: s64, point: s64) -> s64 {
    if point < 128 {
        out[write_at] = cast(u8, point);
        return 1;
    }
    if point < 2048 {
        out[write_at] = cast(u8, 192 + point / 64);
        out[write_at + 1] = cast(u8, 128 + point % 64);
        return 2;
    }
    if point < 65536 {
        out[write_at] = cast(u8, 224 + point / 4096);
        out[write_at + 1] = cast(u8, 128 + point / 64 % 64);
        out[write_at + 2] = cast(u8, 128 + point % 64);
        return 3;
    }
    out[write_at] = cast(u8, 240 + point / 262144);
    out[write_at + 1] = cast(u8, 128 + point / 4096 % 64);
    out[write_at + 2] = cast(u8, 128 + point / 64 % 64);
    out[write_at + 3] = cast(u8, 128 + point % 64);
    return 4;
}

/// libc `strtod(3)`, for the exact decimal-to-`float64` conversion.
///
/// Reachable because ADR-0114 let a float cross the `#foreign` boundary. The `end` out-parameter is ignored
/// here — this module has already found the number's extent with its own scanner, which has to happen anyway
/// to reject what JSON forbids and C accepts (`0x10`, `inf`, a leading `+`).
strtod :: (text: *u8, end: **u8) -> float64 #foreign libc "strtod";

/// Parses a number, exactly.
///
/// The **extent is found by JSON's grammar** and the **value by `strtod`**, and that split is the decision:
/// `strtod` alone would accept `0x1p3`, `inf`, `nan` and a leading `+`, none of which are JSON, and a
/// hand-written conversion would be a rounding bug waiting to happen (the argument `Math` makes about
/// `sqrt`, ADR-0112). So the scanner says what a number *is* and libc says what it *equals*.
///
/// An integral number also gets an exact `s64`, computed here rather than from the float, because a
/// `float64` cannot hold every `s64` above 2^53 and a caller indexing an array wants the value they wrote.
/// Overflow past `s64` leaves `is_integer` false, so the float remains the only answer — which is honest,
/// since the exact integer genuinely does not fit.
parse_number :: (doc: *Json_Document, text: string, at: s64) -> (s64, bool, s64) {
    cursor := at;
    negative := false;
    if byte_at(text, cursor) == 45 {
        negative = true;
        cursor = cursor + 1;
    }

    // An integer part is required, and a leading zero may not be followed by a digit: `01` is not JSON.
    digits_start := cursor;
    if cursor >= text.count {
        return NONE, false, cursor;
    }
    if byte_at(text, cursor) == 48 {
        cursor = cursor + 1;
        if cursor < text.count {
            if is_digit(byte_at(text, cursor)) {
                return NONE, false, cursor;
            }
        }
    } else {
        if !is_digit(byte_at(text, cursor)) {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        while cursor < text.count {
            if !is_digit(byte_at(text, cursor)) {
                break;
            }
            cursor = cursor + 1;
        }
    }
    integer_end := cursor;

    integral := true;
    if cursor < text.count {
        if byte_at(text, cursor) == 46 {
            integral = false;
            cursor = cursor + 1;
            // At least one digit after the point: `1.` is not JSON.
            if cursor >= text.count {
                return NONE, false, cursor;
            }
            if !is_digit(byte_at(text, cursor)) {
                return NONE, false, cursor;
            }
            while cursor < text.count {
                if !is_digit(byte_at(text, cursor)) {
                    break;
                }
                cursor = cursor + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    if cursor < text.count {
        e := byte_at(text, cursor);
        if e == 101 {
            integral = false;
            cursor = cursor + 1;
        } else {
            if e == 69 {
                integral = false;
                cursor = cursor + 1;
            }
        }
    }
    if !integral {
        if cursor < text.count {
            sign := byte_at(text, cursor);
            if sign == 43 {
                cursor = cursor + 1;
            } else {
                if sign == 45 {
                    cursor = cursor + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        // Only when an exponent marker was actually consumed does a digit become required; a fraction with
        // no exponent has already been fully scanned. The check is `cursor > integer_end + 1` rather than a
        // flag, because the fraction path leaves `cursor` past the point either way.
        if !exponent_well_formed(text, cursor, integer_end) {
            return NONE, false, cursor;
        }
        while cursor < text.count {
            if !is_digit(byte_at(text, cursor)) {
                break;
            }
            cursor = cursor + 1;
        }
    }

    node := add_node(doc, Json_Kind.NUMBER);
    if node == NONE {
        return NONE, false, at;
    }
    nodes := view(doc.nodes.data, doc.nodes.count);
    nodes[node].number = decimal_value(text, at, cursor);
    if integral {
        value, fits := integer_value(text, digits_start, integer_end);
        if fits {
            nodes[node].is_integer = true;
            if negative {
                nodes[node].integer = 0 - value;
            } else {
                nodes[node].integer = value;
            }
        }
    }
    return node, true, cursor;
}

/// Whether the exponent starting at `cursor` has its required digit.
///
/// Split out because the condition reads badly inline: an exponent needs a digit, and a plain fraction has
/// none to need. `integer_end` distinguishes them — a fraction-only number has already consumed everything.
exponent_well_formed :: (text: string, cursor: s64, integer_end: s64) -> bool {
    if cursor >= text.count {
        return integer_end < cursor;
    }
    if is_digit(byte_at(text, cursor)) {
        return true;
    }
    // No digit here is fine only if there was no exponent marker — in which case the previous byte is a
    // digit of the fraction rather than `e`, `+` or `-`.
    return !exponent_marker_before(text, cursor);
}

/// Whether an `e`/`E` (possibly with a sign after it) sits just before `cursor`.
exponent_marker_before :: (text: string, cursor: s64) -> bool {
    back := cursor - 1;
    if back < 0 {
        return false;
    }
    c := byte_at(text, back);
    if c == 43 {
        back = back - 1;
    } else {
        if c == 45 {
            back = back - 1;
        }
    }
    if back < 0 {
        return false;
    }
    m := byte_at(text, back);
    if m == 101 {
        return true;
    }
    return m == 69;
}

/// Whether `c` is an ASCII digit.
is_digit :: (c: s64) -> bool {
    if c < 48 {
        return false;
    }
    return c <= 57;
}

/// The exact `s64` the digits in `[from, to)` spell, and whether it fit.
///
/// Compared against the limit **before** multiplying, so overflow is detected rather than wrapped — a
/// wrapped count is a silently wrong answer, which is the failure mode this project refuses everywhere else.
integer_value :: (text: string, from: s64, to: s64) -> (s64, bool) {
    // 922337203685477580 is `s64`'s maximum divided by ten, so a value above it cannot be multiplied.
    limit :: 922337203685477580;
    value := 0;
    i := from;
    while i < to {
        digit := byte_at(text, i) - 48;
        if value > limit {
            return 0, false;
        }
        value = value * 10;
        if value > 9223372036854775807 - digit {
            return 0, false;
        }
        value = value + digit;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return value, true;
}

/// The `float64` the source slice `[from, to)` equals, via `strtod`.
///
/// The slice is copied into a NUL-terminated scratch buffer from **temporary storage**, because `strtod`
/// reads to a NUL and a Jairs `string` is data and a count with no terminator. The arena is right here for
/// the same reason it is right for a sort's scratch (ADR-0155 §3): the copy is dead the moment the call
/// returns.
decimal_value :: (text: string, from: s64, to: s64) -> float64 {
    n := to - from;
    scratch := talloc(n + 1);
    if scratch == null {
        return 0.0;
    }
    bytes := view(scratch, n + 1);
    i := 0;
    while i < n {
        bytes[i] = cast(u8, byte_at(text, from + i));
        i = i + 1;
    }
    bytes[n] = cast(u8, 0);
    ignored: *u8;
    return strtod(scratch, *ignored);
}
